WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:04.289
All right, so what I have is basically a case of you can't believe your eyes. So,

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.750
you know in a lot of the stuff that we look at in maths

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:07.500
we look for patterns?

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:07.980
Right?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.960
And we try it for a few examples and if we see a pattern then we think, oh maybe this continues on, right? But it's

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.500
not like a proof. It's not a convincing argument.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.859
And so what I want to talk about today is an example of when the pattern just completely

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.080
misleads us. If you take, like, a random number,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.240
is it more likely to have an odd number of prime factors or an even number of prime factors?

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:34.168
That's the basic question. Say 10 - the way that it breaks down into prime numbers is that it's the product of 2

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:38.309
which is prime, and 5 which is also prime. So it has two prime factors,

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:42.750
okay, so it has an even number of prime factors. But of course if I take any prime number,

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.550
like say 5 or 2 or whatever then it only has one prime factor,

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.230
and so it has an odd number of prime factors.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:54.270
You're gonna see when I write it down in a minute that I'm gonna ignore, totally, the numbers that have repeated prime factors.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.980
So I'm gonna define a function and I'm gonna use this notation, because all the mathematicians will yell at me if I don't!

00:01:01.340 --> 00:01:02.199
[laughs]
(Brady: What is that?)

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:08.338
So this is the letter mu, and what this function is gonna do is it's gonna take in a number, and it's gonna output

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:14.159
1 if n - my number - has an even number of prime factors.

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:20.820
So like 10, we talked about that example, because 2 &amp; 5 are its prime factors, but also none repeated.

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:25.679
It's gonna give me the value minus 1 if all this stuff is still true;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.749
except I have an odd number of prime factors.

00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:32.008
Any prime number would be given the assignment of the value minus 1

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.860
under this function. We'll give it the value 0 if a prime is repeated when we factor the number.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.940
Let's do some hands-on work here, because this is kind of a complicated definition.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.420
(Brady: Does it get a zero if the prime - 'cause sometimes you get a factorisation where a certain factor is repeated?)

00:01:47.549 --> 00:01:49.500
- Yeah
- (Brady: And another one just appears once.)
- Ah, yeah

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.000
(Brady: Like, you know, 2 times 2 times 2 times 5.)
- Yeah. Yeah. 
- (Brady: That would count as a zero?)

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.389
So that would count as a zero - if any time we have a repeat then we assign zero to this thing.

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:02.070
Yeah, let's look at some, some concrete examples, because I think it's easy once you start working with it,

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:04.000
but hard to talk about abstractly.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.928
Okay, so here's my numbers, and I'll look at the values of this function, mu. So let's forget about zero,

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.969
I don't want to even think about it. Let's start with 2.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.370
Okay, that's a prime number. It has one prime factor, which is an odd number,

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:19.419
so we assign it a minus 1. 3, same deal. 4,

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.279
this is the bad news situation where we have a repeated prime factor, because it's 2 times 2.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.910
So we assign it 0.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:32.739
5 is prime, it gets a minus 1. 6 factors into 2 times 3. That's two; number of prime factors,

00:02:32.739 --> 00:02:36.579
and so we assign it a 1. So let me just fill in a few more here.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.730
8 takes zero,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:47.809
9 is a square, it takes zero. 10 - we already talked about it has two prime factors. And you can keep going, right?

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.749
Alright, so if I want to know which happens more often

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.619
all I have to do is add up the values of this function.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.860
'Cause it's negative when I have an odd number of prime factors,

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:00.529
it's positive when I have an even number of prime factors -

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:05.239
what I should do is just add up the minus 1s and the plus 1s and just see if I get something positive or negative

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:09.739
(Brady: And which side of the ledger are you on?)
- Which side of the ledger am I on. And this will tell me what's going on.

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.810
What do I get when I add up these numbers?

00:03:16.709 --> 00:03:19.309
So in the first initial computation,

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:25.130
there's a few more numbers with an odd number of prime factors than an even number of prime factors.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.130
So I don't have an answer for you,
[laughs]

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:33.290
about like what happens more often because, it's a little - you have to be a little careful to formulate what you mean.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.890
Because you're sort of doing this trade-off going back and forth between

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:40.730
minus 1 and plus 1, and - you can always sort of find another prime number

00:03:40.730 --> 00:03:43.429
so there's a minus 1. But then okay, you can multiply that 1 by 2,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.970
so then there's a plus 1. So you keep going through all of the whole numbers

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:52.309
and you're gonna get lots of minus 1s and you're gonna get lots of 1s. So there's a lot of cancellation.

00:03:52.769 --> 00:03:58.819
Ok? And so what I mean is that this sum should always be pretty small.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:06.470
Minus 2 is smaller than 7, by a decent amount. You can imagine as you keep going on the numbers get really big

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.720
but the sum doesn't get so big. 
- (Brady: Is it gonna hover around zero, like?)
- So that's the question.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:16.940
So first of all, you can show that it's not gonna hover around zero. It's gonna zigzag like crazy actually.

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:19.440
But, the conjecture,

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.979
the thing that people thought was true for a long time,

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:26.869
was that it's never gonna be larger than the square root of the number that you've gone up to,

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.269
So if I do this and I go up through this process of adding together all these minus 1s and plus 1s, and say I

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:33.660
stop at a million;

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:38.539
then the thought is well, okay, whatever number I get, it's some number between minus

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:44.239
1,000 and positive 1,000; 'cause that's the square root of a million.
- (Brady: But it could be either side of zero?)

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.159
It could be either side of zero. And in fact, it is known that it switches back and forth,

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:54.609
infinitely often. So, that's like a really complicated like thing to think about, yeah!
- (Brady: That's amazing too!)

00:04:55.189 --> 00:04:59.709
But - 
- (Brady:  So it could get really high? It could get up into the thousands and then it could drop to the minus a thousand?

00:04:59.710 --> 00:04:59.979
That's right, yeah,

00:05:00.219 --> 00:05:05.709
and just zigzagging back and forth, sort of living around zero but in a really complicated way.

00:05:06.199 --> 00:05:12.459
And so, the thought was that - okay, it's complicated but, because I have all this cancellation it can never be too big;

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.090
it has to be between minus the square root of the number, and the square root of the number.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:21.340
So this is known as Mertens conjecture, and because this is called Mertens function - this sum that I'm adding up. And it was thought to

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:26.619
be true for a long time and it turns out that it's not true. But it's crazy because if you do this,

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:30.729
I mean we can now compute really far, and you go billions and billions and billions of

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:36.729
numbers out - and it still satisfies this square root bound. What is now known is that there is a

00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:43.059
counter example to this conjecture. You eventually zigzag out past the square root of the number, but we also know that

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:48.639
we have some terrible terrible bounds on how big the number has to be to actually do this.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:52.299
So it's something like 10 to the 10 to the 40. Something like that.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.569
(Brady: So do we know the exact number where it first breaks the rule?)

00:05:55.569 --> 00:05:56.050
Well,

00:05:56.050 --> 00:06:01.200
I mean if we knew it we could never write it down, because we would need all of the atoms in the universe to write it down

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:02.719
[laughs]
- (Brady: Aww yeah)

00:06:02.719 --> 00:06:07.689
So no, the answer is no we don't know. Like we'd have no way to describe the first time that it happened

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:09.849
(Brady: But we know it exists?)
- But we know it exists.

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:15.669
So, despite all computational evidence that could ever be produced in the history and future life of the universe;

00:06:16.219 --> 00:06:18.338
we know that it still fails to be true. So

00:06:19.069 --> 00:06:24.968
what we're looking at is a picture of this function, where we're adding up these minus 1s and plus 1s and going along with the

00:06:24.969 --> 00:06:27.609
integers. And so, as you can see, what's happening

00:06:27.610 --> 00:06:30.039
is that the - as we add more and more terms to the sum,

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:35.408
we go back and forth - here's zero - we go back and forth around zero - we start kind of more negative, by the way.

00:06:35.419 --> 00:06:38.588
That's kind of fun to notice, because there's lots of prime numbers that are small.

00:06:39.139 --> 00:06:44.258
So, we go back and forth around zero and, but, no matter how much back and forth we do

00:06:44.259 --> 00:06:49.088
we're not getting anywhere close to this boundary given by the square root of the number.

00:06:49.129 --> 00:06:53.889
So we zigzag back and forth positive and negative, positive and negative. But no matter how large we get

00:06:53.889 --> 00:06:55.958
we don't even come close to approaching that boundary.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:07:00.949
(Brady: You can't draw that graph for the point where it gets nearer the square root because numbers is too big?)

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:02.599
Yeah, that's right. The numbers are too big

00:07:02.599 --> 00:07:02.930
I mean,

00:07:02.930 --> 00:07:06.680
they just can't even be written down much less computed.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:12.949
(Brady: But one day that that's zigzag, that Alps mountain range will break free of that square root loop)

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.650
It will, but I don't think we're gonna be around to see it.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:17.550
[laughs]

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:22.819
Unfortunately.
- (Brady: Does it *just* break away from that square root rule? Or does it blast past it?)

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:29.269
So that's a really complicated question. We don't know the answer to that we know it doesn't go too far past it

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.229
but actually the reason why people were initially - or one

00:07:33.449 --> 00:07:40.248
interesting thing initially noticed about this question: is that if it had been true, if this false thing had been true, it would imply the

00:07:40.249 --> 00:07:46.399
Riemann Hypothesis. Which is this really big, open question in mathematics that's thought to be like very difficult and very strong.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:53.599
So understanding the growth of the, the size of this Mertens function is actually really, intimately connected to some of the most difficult

00:07:53.699 --> 00:08:00.199
problems in mathematics. And I think there was at some point some hope of proving the Riemann hypothesis by proving this conjecture,

00:08:00.599 --> 00:08:02.599
so when it was shown to be false

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:04.140
we lost that hope.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:09.500
(Brady: I love that there's some number floating out there, when the rule first gets broken, but we could never even write it down.)

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.819
Yeah, that's right, I mean

00:08:10.819 --> 00:08:12.509
we could never even arrange - I

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:16.459
keep going back to atoms in the universe, because that's what makes me sort of happy and blows my mind.

00:08:16.500 --> 00:08:22.250
Right, we could never even arrange all of the atoms in the universe to describe the - like they could never be lined up in any

00:08:22.710 --> 00:08:27.319
computable way. But we know it's there 
- (Brady: And it's like finite? It's an exact number?)
- Yeah. Yeah,

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.400
it's - the only way we can describe it as the first number that fails Mertens' conjecture.

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.600
(Brady: Has that got a name? Is it, is it called like his - his number or something?)

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:37.600
No, that's a good point. I don't think it has a name. Maybe we should name it

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.640
(Brady: That's my new favourite number)

